A diagram $\Gamma$ is called an \emph{extended Dynkin tree diagram} if 
\begin{itemize} 
\item 
$\Gamma$ is a tree diagram with edge weights $\leq 3$; 
\item 
$\Gamma$ is not on the Dynkin diagram list; 
\item 
every proper subdiagram of $\Gamma$ is a disjoint union of Dynkin diagrams. 
\end{itemize} 
(In this definition, we ignore the orientations of the edges.) 
To prove the proposition, it is enough to show that any extended 
Dynkin tree diagram is $2$-infinite. 
Direct inspection shows that Figure~\ref{fig:extended-dynkin-diagrams} 
provides a complete list of such diagrams. 
Here each tree $X_n^{(1)}$ has $n+1$ vertices. 
As before, all unspecified edge weights are equal to $1$; in the 
diagram~$G_2^{(1)}$, we have $a\in\{1,2,3\}$. 
We note that all these diagrams are associated with untwisted affine Lie algebras 
and can be found in the tables in \cite{bourbaki} or in 
\cite[Chapter 4, Table Aff~1]{kac}. 
The only diagram from those tables that is missing in 
Figure~\ref{fig:extended-dynkin-diagrams} is $A_n^{(1)}$, which is 
an $(n+1)$-cycle; it will be treated in Section~\ref{sec:take-care-of-cycles}. 

